// In-depth battles, batch o: otumba, tenochtitlan, mactan, panipat, mohacs, cajamarca.
// Checked against the English, Spanish, Portuguese and (for Mohacs) Hungarian Wikipedia articles on each battle and
// their leaders (Cortes, Cuauhtemoc, Magellan, Lapulapu, Babur, Ibrahim Lodi, Louis II, Tomori, Pizarro, Atahualpa),
// plus the Hungarian Ministry of Defence history article on Mohacs, HistoryOfWar.org and the EBSCO Research Starters on
// Panipat and Cajamarca, and the Warfare History Network article on Otumba. The works those pages cite (Diaz del
// Castillo, Gomara, Pigafetta, Chandra, Sarkar, Hemming, MacQuarrie, Brodarics, Perjes, Hassig, Thomas) were read only
// through those summaries. Britannica and the Pigafetta text itself could not be opened.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "conquistador": "A Spanish soldier or adventurer who, in the 16th century, conquered lands in the Americas in the name of the king of Spain, almost always on his own account and in search of gold and glory.",
  "tlaxcaltecas": "The people of Tlaxcala, an independent region that the Aztecs never managed to conquer. After fighting Cortés, they allied with him and supplied most of the soldiers who conquered Mexico.",
  "cihuacóatl": "A high office in the Aztec government, one of the city's main authorities, ranking below the emperor.",
  "tlatoani": "The supreme ruler of an Aztec city. The one of Tenochtitlan was the emperor.",
  "varíola": "A contagious and very serious disease that leaves sores on the skin. Europeans brought it to the Americas, where nobody had any defense against it, and it killed a great many people.",
  "noite triste": "The name of the Spaniards' flight from Tenochtitlan, on the night of 30 June to 1 July 1520. They lost many men, all their cannons and much of their gold.",
  "arcabuz": "A portable 16th-century firearm with a long barrel, lit with a match. It was slow to reload and not very accurate, but it frightened anyone who had never heard a gunshot.",
  "bergantim": "A light, fast boat with sails and oars and a cannon at the bow. Cortés had thirteen of them built to master the lake around Tenochtitlan.",
  "calçada": "A wide, raised embankment that linked the island of Tenochtitlan to the lakeshore. It had gaps covered by wooden bridges, which could be removed in a defense.",
  "recife": "A ridge of rock or coral just below the water, near the coast. It keeps boats from getting close to the beach.",
  "datu": "A local chief in the Visayas islands of the Philippines in the 16th century. He ruled a settlement or a group of them, without being a king in the European style.",
  "mosquete": "A long-barreled firearm fired from the shoulder. The oldest kind, the matchlock, needed a lit fuse to fire, and so was slow to prepare.",
  "mantelete": "A large wooden shield, propped on the ground or set on wheels, behind which shooters took cover.",
  "tulughma": "A Turco-Mongol tactic of envelopment: fast cavalry wings ride around the enemy's sides and attack from the rear, while the center holds him in front.",
  "mogóis": "The Muslim dynasty founded by Babur, which ruled much of India from 1526. The name comes from 'Mongol', because Babur descended from Genghis Khan.",
  "voivoda": "A title for a military governor in parts of Central and Eastern Europe. The voivode of Transylvania ran that province on behalf of the king of Hungary.",
  "akinci": "A light, irregular horseman of the Ottoman army. He raided ahead of the army to plunder, spread fear and surround the enemy.",
  "requerimento": "A text the Spaniards read to the peoples of the Americas before attacking. It demanded that they accept the king of Spain and the Christian faith and, if they refused, warned that they would be fought. Almost nobody understood it.",
  "liteira": "A chair or platform carried on the shoulders of several men, in which an important person was transported.",
  "falconete": "A small, light cannon that fired balls a few centimeters across.",
  "garrote": "A method of execution by strangulation, with a rope or an iron ring tightened around the neck. It was considered less cruel than burning at the stake.",
  "curaca": "A local chief among the Incas, responsible for a village or a region and for the labor it owed to the state."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  otumba: {
    war: "Conquest of Mexico by Hernán Cortés (1519–1521)",
    hook: "Wounded, starving and without cannons, a few hundred Spaniards were surrounded on a plain by an entire army, and everything was decided in a charge against a single man.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1519, Hernán Cortés landed on the coast of Mexico with a few hundred [[conquistadors|conquistador]]. After fighting the [[Tlaxcalans|tlaxcaltecas]], he made them his allies, and with them he marched to Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital, in the middle of a lake. In November 1519 he entered the city, welcomed by the emperor Moctezuma II, and soon turned him into a hostage.",
        "In 1520, everything fell apart. The governor of Cuba, Diego Velázquez, sent Pánfilo de Narváez to arrest Cortés, and Cortés left the city to defeat him, in May, and took his men into his own force. While he was away, Pedro de Alvarado attacked Aztec nobles during a religious ceremony, and the city rose in revolt. Cortés came back to a capital at war.",
        { h: "The Noche Triste" },
        "On the night of 30 June to 1 July 1520, the Spaniards tried to escape along the Tlacopan causeway, in the rain. They were discovered and attacked by warriors on foot and in canoes. They lost all their cannons, about 46 horses and much of the treasure, and many soldiers drowned weighed down with gold. How many died, nobody knows: Cortés speaks of 154 Spaniards and more than 2,000 allies; Bernal Díaz del Castillo, of about 450 in one passage and 860 in another (including those lost at Otumba); Gómara, of 450 Spaniards and 4,000 allies.",
        { h: "The road to Otumba" },
        { timeline: [
          ["November 1519", "Cortés enters Tenochtitlan and is welcomed by Moctezuma II."],
          ["May 1520", "He defeats Narváez and returns to a city in revolt."],
          ["30 June to 1 July 1520", "The [[Noche Triste|noite triste]]: the flight along the Tlacopan causeway."],
          ["About a week", "A flight march, north and then east, toward Tlaxcala, pursued all the way."],
          ["7 July 1520", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The fugitives rested at Popotla and at Tlacopan and then made a loop to the north of the lake, passing through Cuautitlán and Tepotzotlán, to avoid the most populated places. Near Zumpango, they killed a horse and ate it: it was the first meat in days. All around, Mexica warriors attacked them without stopping. They had to reach Tlaxcala, to the east, and to do that they needed to cross the plain of Otumba."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Hernán Cortés, about 35 years old" },
        "Born around 1485 in Medellín, in Extremadura, he had reached Mexico with almost no soldiers and had taken a capital. Now he had a wounded head, no cannons and almost no horses. What saved him at Otumba was a piece of advice that, according to one version, came from the Tlaxcalans: among the Aztecs, a battle was decided when the commander fell and his standard was taken.",
        { h: "Matlatzincátzin, the Mexica commander" },
        "Almost nothing is known about him. The sources call him [[cihuacoatl|cihuacóatl]], one of the highest offices in the Aztec government, and English Wikipedia also describes him, in one passage, as a high priest. He commanded the army that came to meet the fugitives, and he carried the feathered standard that made him recognizable from afar.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Spanish side, the cavalry captains who, according to the accounts, charged with Cortés: Gonzalo de Sandoval, Cristóbal de Olid, Pedro de Alvarado and Alonso Dávila. And Juan de Salamanca, the man with the lance, who is discussed at the end. On the indigenous side, the warriors of Tlaxcala, whom Bernal Díaz praises for fighting 'like lions'."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are very uncertain. The Spaniards, according to historians, numbered between 340 (Hugh Thomas), 440 (Bernal Díaz) and 600 (Chavero), including a few women, and with few horsemen (the sources say 13 to 20). They had almost no firearms: one of the lists mentions 7 arquebuses and 12 crossbows. With them came Tlaxcalans, fewer than a thousand according to most historians; Chavero went as high as 3,000.",
        "The Mexica were far more. The chroniclers Gómara, Solís and Alva Ixtlilxóchitl speak of about 200,000, with 20,000 dead. Nineteenth-century historians, such as Orozco y Berra and Chavero, found these numbers exaggerated. Today the most cited ranges run from 10,000 to 20,000 warriors. Bernal Díaz and Cortés himself describe a confused multitude, without giving a number.",
        { note: "The numbers for Otumba depend almost entirely on accounts by those who fought on the Spanish side. Exact counts do not exist, and the ranges above are those of historians, not of the chroniclers." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open plain near present-day Otumba, in the Valley of Mexico, known as Temalcatitlán (or Tonampoco). In open country, the Mexica advantage in numbers was total, because they could surround the Spaniards on all sides.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "When the Spaniards reached the plain, they found a large Mexica army. The warriors, though more numerous and facing exhausted men, did not attack at once.",
        "In combat, the Mexica tried to take their enemies alive in order to sacrifice them, rather than kill them, which, according to Spanish Wikipedia, made coordinated fighting harder. The Spaniards formed a circle, with the pikemen on the outside, and held out for hours, breaking the encirclement several times. Bernal Díaz says it was the cavalry that opened the way, with the rodeleros (soldiers with shield and sword) and the Tlaxcalans behind. Even so, the multitude almost swallowed them.",
        "Then Cortés saw the feathered standard and the commander's armor, and called the nearest horsemen to follow him. He and his captains charged straight at that spot. According to Spanish Wikipedia, Cortés knocked Matlatzincátzin down, and Juan de Salamanca killed him with the lance and took the feathers and the standard, which he handed to Cortés. Other versions say it was Cortés himself who killed him.",
        "Without a commander and without a standard, the Mexica army broke up and fell back. The Spaniards and the Tlaxcalans pursued the fugitives, and took armor, shields and feathers. An Aztec chronicle cited by the Warfare History Network describes the slaughter of those who were fleeing."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Spaniards lost about 60 men, according to English Wikipedia (the Spanish one says 73). Among the Tlaxcalans, there were many dead and wounded, with no number given. Mexica losses are unknown, and the chroniclers speak of 20,000. Cortés came out wounded in the head and the hand.",
        "The next day, 8 July, the survivors reached Tlaxcala. They rested for about twenty days. The chiefs of Tlaxcala refused a peace offer from the new emperor, Cuitláhuac, and remained allies of Cortés. From there, he would organize the return, which would end with the fall of Tenochtitlan in August 1521.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "If Otumba had been lost, the Spaniards would have died on the plain, and the conquest of Mexico would have ended there. For the historian Juan Miralles, cited in Spanish Wikipedia, it was the point where the tide turned, an opinion that even Cortés's harshest critics tend to share. King Charles I later allowed Salamanca to put the feathers of the standard on his coat of arms.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: the chroniclers do not agree. Cortés implies it was 8 July, the historians Orozco y Berra, Chavero and Pereyra use 7 July, and Bernal Díaz says 14 July. The calendar of the time was the Julian one; in the Gregorian, the 7th would be 17 July." },
        { note: "Who killed the commander: Cortés, Salamanca or another horseman, depending on the account. The Codex Ramírez, for its part, gives credit for much of the fighting to Ixtlilxóchitl, of Texcoco, and speaks of Cortés killing a single captain, without facing overwhelming odds." },
        { note: "The sources: the fight is described by Cortés (in his letters to the king), by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, by Gómara (who was not even there) and by indigenous accounts. Each one had reasons to inflate his own part." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  tenochtitlan: {
    war: "Conquest of Mexico by Hernán Cortés (1519–1521)",
    hook: "A capital built in the middle of a lake fell after two and a half months of siege, an epidemic and an alliance of peoples tired of Aztec rule.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital, stood on an island in Lake Texcoco, linked to the shore by raised [[causeways|calçada]]. In July 1520, after the Noche Triste and the victory at Otumba, Cortés reached Tlaxcala with almost nothing. In the following months, the balance shifted for three reasons.",
        "The first was disease. At the end of October 1520, a [[smallpox|varíola]] epidemic began in Tenochtitlan and lasted about sixty days. It killed many people, among them the new emperor, Cuitláhuac, who had ruled for about eighty days. The disease is estimated to have killed about 40% of the native population of the region in one year. According to English Wikipedia, the Spaniards, in general, fell ill far less because they had acquired immunity. The second was succession: at the start of 1521, Cuauhtémoc, about 25 years old, was chosen [[tlatoani|tlatoani]]. The third was Cortés's preparation: he had thirteen [[brigantines|bergantim]] built in Tlaxcala, to master the lake.",
        { h: "The road to the fall" },
        { timeline: [
          ["July 1520", "After Otumba, the Spaniards reach Tlaxcala and recover."],
          ["End of October 1520", "The smallpox epidemic begins in Tenochtitlan. Cuitláhuac dies."],
          ["Early 1521", "Cuauhtémoc is chosen tlatoani."],
          ["End of April 1521", "Cortés gathers the troops and the thirteen brigantines at Texcoco."],
          ["22 to 26 May 1521", "The Spaniards take position on the causeways. The siege begins."],
          ["30 June", "Cortés loses many men in an attack on Tlatelolco."],
          ["July and August", "Reinforcements arrive. The Spaniards take the city district by district."],
          ["13 August 1521", "Cuauhtémoc is captured. The city surrenders."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Hernán Cortés, about 36 years old" },
        "He had everything to lose, because he had disobeyed the governor of Cuba. His strategy was patient: not to attack the island head-on, but to surround it, cut off food and water, win the lake and let time and disease weaken the city. On entering each district, he had the houses torn down and used the rubble to fill the canals, so that the infantry and the cavalry could advance in formation.",
        { h: "Cuauhtémoc, about 25 years old" },
        "The last Aztec emperor. He came to power with the city already besieged by disease and war, and he defended it to the end. He was captured on 13 August 1521, as he tried to flee the city by canoe. According to Spanish sources, he asked Cortés to kill him, and Cortés replied that he had defended his capital like a brave warrior.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Pedro de Alvarado commanded the camp at Tlacopan; Cristóbal de Olid, the one at Coyoacán; Gonzalo de Sandoval, the one at Iztapalapa. Cortés took charge of the brigantines. On the indigenous side, the great ally was Tlaxcala, but also Texcoco, Chalco and Huexotzinco. Bernal Díaz gives special credit for the victory to Malinche, or Doña Marina, Cortés's interpreter."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The figures for Cortés's forces, on Wikipedia, are: 900 to 1,300 Spanish infantry soldiers, 86 horsemen (90 to 100 in another version), 16 cannons, 13 brigantines and 50,000 to 200,000 indigenous allies. The range for the allies is enormous, and nobody knows for sure. On the Aztec side, the number cited is about 80,000 warriors and 400 war canoes, with no clear source.",
        { note: "The numbers for this campaign come from a few chroniclers, such as Cortés and Bernal Díaz del Castillo, and vary greatly from one source to another." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An island city, surrounded by water on all sides and linked to the shore by causeways. Attacking by land meant going along the causeways, where enemy canoes could attack the soldiers on both sides. Without control of the lake, the Spaniards would be trapped on the causeways.",
        { h: "Taking position" },
        "On 22 May 1521, the Spaniards occupied their positions: Alvarado at Tlacopan, Olid at Coyoacán, Sandoval at Iztapalapa. Alvarado and Olid went first to Chapultepec, from where part of the city's water came by an aqueduct, and cut it. Each captain had about 8,000 allies (Alvarado and Olid from Tlaxcala, Sandoval from Chalco and Huexotzinco), according to Bernal Díaz's counts.",
        { h: "The lake" },
        "Cortés launched the brigantines from Texcoco. More than a thousand canoes came out to meet them, according to Bernal Díaz, but a favorable wind let the boats overturn many of them. Cortés opened a breach in a causeway so the brigantines could pass from one side to the other, and the Aztecs lost the flank attack. But the Aztecs placed stakes and traps in the shallows, captured two boats and killed the captains Juan de la Portilla and Pedro Barba. An ambush of thirty canoes was answered by a counter-ambush of six boats.",
        { h: "Colhuacatonco: the setback" },
        "On 30 June (Wikipedia gives 24 June in another article), Cortés sent a large attack on the Tlatelolco market, in three columns. He advanced, but stopped to check whether the gaps in the causeway had been filled in, and the Aztecs, hidden in the houses, attacked. Cuauhtémoc sounded a great horn and ordered them to fight until victory or death. Cortés was wounded and almost captured, and Cristóbal de Olea died saving him. About 40 to 66 Spaniards were taken prisoner (Gómara says 40, Sahagún's informants 53, Bernal Díaz 66) and sacrificed in the following days; about 2,000 allies died. The Aztecs sent heads to the Spanish camps and to rebel cities, to show that the Spaniards could be beaten.",
        "Many allies thought the war was lost and left. Ixtlilxóchitl II, of Texcoco, and the Tlaxcalan commander stayed and urged Cortés to continue. He spent four days without attacking. Then the Spaniards learned to destroy the stakes, retook the lake, and many allies returned.",
        { h: "The end" },
        "In mid-July, gunpowder and reinforcements arrived. The Spaniards entered the Tlatelolco market around 1 August and took it four days later. In the city, the water turned brackish and dysentery spread. On 13 August, Cuauhtémoc tried to flee by canoe with his wife, his family and friends, and was captured. The city surrendered the same day."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Spanish losses appear as 450 to 860 dead in Wikipedia's lists; other accounts speak of only 40 dead in the siege, or a little over 100, and Gómara estimates 450. The indigenous allies lost about 20,000 men, according to an estimate with no clear source. On the Aztec side, estimates run from 100,000 to 240,000 dead, warriors and civilians together, and it is impossible to separate those who died of smallpox, of hunger or in the fighting.",
        "After the surrender, the indigenous allies went on killing and looting. Cortés wrote to the king that his men could not prevent the Tlaxcalans from killing and sacrificing more than 15,000 people that day. Almost the entire Aztec nobility was dead, and the survivors were mostly young women and children.",
        "Cortés demanded the gold lost on the Noche Triste. Cuauhtémoc and the lord of Tacuba, tortured with fire, said they had thrown it into the lake, but almost nothing was found. Cuauhtémoc was kept as a figurehead chief until 1525, when Cortés had him hanged during an expedition to Honduras, accused of plotting a conspiracy. The accounts of Ixtlilxóchitl, a descendant of the Texcoco nobility, say the conspiracy was invented.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Aztec Empire came to an end. From the rubble rose Mexico City, capital of New Spain, and Spanish rule later spread across the rest of the Americas. For some historians, such as Israel Charny, John Cox and Norman Naimark, the siege was a genocide. This is an interpretation. Portuguese Wikipedia recalls that most of the deaths among the indigenous people came from the diseases brought by the Europeans, such as smallpox, and not from the fighting.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The role of the allies: most of the soldiers who took Tenochtitlan were from indigenous peoples hostile to the Aztecs. It is hard to imagine a Spanish victory without them, and what is called the 'Spanish conquest' was also, in large part, a war between indigenous peoples." },
        { note: "The losses: Cortés claimed to have lost only 15 Spaniards, a number that Wikipedia presents as his own claim and that is not accepted. Gómara says 450." },
        { note: "The death of Cuauhtémoc: the Spanish accounts (Cortés, Bernal Díaz, who thought the execution unjust) and the indigenous ones (Ixtlilxóchitl) do not agree on whether there was a real conspiracy." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  mactan: {
    war: "The voyage of Magellan and Elcano, the first circumnavigation (1519–1522)",
    hook: "The commander of the first voyage around the world died on a beach in the Philippines, in a fight he had gone looking for himself.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The expedition set out from Spain in 1519, under the command of the Portuguese navigator Ferdinand Magellan, in the service of the Spanish king. It was looking for a route to the spice islands, the Moluccas, by sailing west. Five ships left, with about 270 men. One ship was wrecked in the winter, another deserted in the strait that now bears Magellan's name, and in March 1521 the other three reached the Philippines.",
        { h: "The road to Mactan" },
        { timeline: [
          ["16 March 1521", "The fleet sights the island of Samar. It is the first documented European arrival in the archipelago."],
          ["28 March", "Arrives at Limasawa. The first Mass in the Philippines is celebrated on 31 March."],
          ["7 April", "Arrives at Cebu."],
          ["April", "Rajah Humabon and the queen are baptized, as Carlos and Juana. Magellan claims to have made about 2,200 converts."],
          ["27 April 1521", "The Battle of Mactan."]
        ]},
        "In Cebu, Magellan made an alliance with Rajah Humabon, who accepted baptism. Neighboring chiefs began to supply food and to convert. But Lapulapu, a [[datu|datu]] of the neighboring island of Mactan, refused to recognize Humabon's authority and to swear loyalty to the king of Spain. According to Pigafetta, who wrote the account, he even prevented another chief of the island, Zula, from sending a son with gifts to Magellan. Humabon and Zula then suggested that Magellan force him to obey. Magellan agreed."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Ferdinand Magellan, about 41 years old" },
        "Born around 1480, he commanded the expedition in the service of the king of Spain. He had found the passage at the south of the Americas, crossed the Pacific and secured the alliance of Cebu. At Mactan, he wanted to force Lapulapu to obey. Beyond the request of Humabon and Zula, the exact motive is debated.",
        { h: "Lapulapu" },
        "Pigafetta calls him 'Çilapulapu', and he is the only direct source who names him. There is no reliable record of his origin. Cebuano oral tradition says his father was the datu Mangal, of Opong, and that he succeeded him. The historian William Henry Scott argues that Magellan misunderstood Visayan society, which was not made up of kingdoms like those of Europe: Lapulapu would have been a powerful datu who owed nothing to Humabon. Almost everything said about him beyond this is tradition or legend.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Humabon and Zula, allies of Magellan, who watched from a distance, at his request. Antonio Pigafetta, an Italian who accompanied the voyage and wrote the main eyewitness account of the battle. Juan Sebastián Elcano, who would later bring the last ship back to Spain."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to Pigafetta, Magellan took about 60 Europeans, of whom 49 landed and 11 stayed on the boats. Humabon offered warriors, and some sources speak of 20 to 30 war boats from Cebu, which Magellan asked to merely watch. The warriors of Mactan numbered about 1,500, according to Pigafetta; other historians say up to 3,000. They used broad swords, shields, bows, iron-tipped spears and fire-hardened poles.",
        { note: "Almost everything known about the battle comes from Pigafetta, who was there. Even so, Filipino historians note that he may have embellished the narrative, and that details such as the warriors' formations may be his." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A beach on Mactan, today part of the province of Cebu. Between the ships and the sand lay a [[reef|recife]], which kept the boats from getting close. The ships' cannons could not reach the beach, and the men had to land in the water and walk to shore, carrying armor and weapons.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Magellan arrived before dawn and sent word to Lapulapu that he could accept the king of Spain and pay tribute, or fight. According to one version, Lapulapu asked that the fight wait for daylight. In the morning, the Spaniards landed and were attacked from the front and from both sides by arrows, spears and stones.",
        "The armor protected most of them, and few died, but the attack wore down their spirits. The crossbowmen and the men with muskets fired from the boats, from a distance, with little effect, until the ammunition ran out, after about half an hour. Magellan ordered houses burned to ease the pressure, which enraged the defenders even more.",
        "It was then that things got worse. The sources differ in details: a poisoned arrow struck him in a leg, where he had no protection, and then a wound to the arm and another, probably from a sword, to the left leg, made him fall. The warriors concentrated on him. His men, according to Pigafetta, disobeyed the order to retreat, and the fight lasted more than an hour. Magellan was killed by spear thrusts. Pigafetta and others managed to escape. Some Spaniards died, and so did some converted Cebuanos who had gone to help."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The warriors of Mactan recovered Magellan's body. Humabon offered, in exchange for it and for the other dead, as many goods as the warriors wanted, but they refused. The death tolls are uncertain: a Filipino source says Pigafetta counted 15 dead on the Mactan side, and English Wikipedia shows 15 and 12 dead without saying on which side.",
        "After the battle, Rajah Humabon turned against the Spaniards. At a banquet, he killed some of those who were left (the accounts speak of poisoning). There were too few men to crew three ships, and the expedition abandoned the Concepción. In November 1521 it reached the Moluccas. The only ship that returned, the Victoria, under Juan Sebastián Elcano, reached Spain on 6 September 1522. Of the about 270 men who had set out, 18 came back.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "For Filipinos, Lapulapu is seen as the first national hero, a symbol of resistance to Spanish rule, although that reading came later: he was a local chief. The Spanish conquest came only in 1564–1565, with the Legazpi expedition, about 44 years later. Today, 27 April is Lapulapu Day, declared in 2017, and the city of Opon was renamed Lapu-Lapu. There is also a monument, the Mactan Shrine, where the battle is reenacted.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Lapulapu's origin: a 1952 book, the Aginid, describes him as a foreigner from Borneo who became a pirate. Most historians treat it as fiction or a hoax. Some accounts from Sulu and Mindanao say he was Muslim, but Pigafetta describes the Visayas as non-Muslim, and most historians reject that idea." },
        { note: "The spelling: Pigafetta writes 'Çilapulapu'. For a long time 'Lapu-Lapu', with a hyphen, was used; the spelling 'Lapulapu', without a hyphen, was recommended in 2019 and officially adopted in 2021." },
        { note: "How Magellan was wounded: the accounts differ. One speaks of a poisoned arrow in the leg, another of a bamboo spear, and English Wikipedia, in one passage, of an arrow in the neck. All agree that he fell after a long fight and was killed by spear thrusts." },
        { note: "The tide: many people say the tide was low. The sources that summarize Pigafetta's account speak of a reef between the ships and the beach, without mentioning the tide." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  panipat: {
    war: "Babur's invasion of northern India (1519–1526)",
    hook: "Twelve thousand men and a row of wagons tied together with rope defeated an army many times larger and opened the way to the Mughal Empire.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Northern India, in 1525, was ruled by the [[Delhi Sultanate|sultanato de déli]], under the Lodi dynasty. The sultan, Ibrahim Lodi, had come to the throne in 1517 and was at war with his own nobles. The [[Rajput|rajputs]] rajas, such as Rana Sanga of Mewar, were rivals. It was a state weak from within, and Babur knew it.",
        "Babur, born on 14 February 1483 in Andijan, descended from Tamerlane on his father's side and from Genghis Khan on his mother's. He had taken Samarkand three times and lost it all three. In 1504, he took Kabul, and from there he began to look to the southeast. He reached the Chenab river, in the Punjab, in 1519. The governor of the Punjab, Daulat Khan Lodi, offered to come over to his side, and an uncle of the sultan, Alam Khan, also sought his help. In 1524, Babur reached Lahore and defeated the Lodi army that came out to meet him.",
        { h: "The road to Panipat" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1504", "Babur takes Kabul."],
          ["1519", "Reaches the Chenab river, in the Punjab."],
          ["1524", "Takes Lahore and defeats the army sent against him."],
          ["December 1525", "Crosses the Indus and marches on Delhi, with about 12,000 men."],
          ["26 February 1526", "His son Humayun defeats a detachment of Ibrahim's at Hisar-Firuza."],
          ["2 April 1526", "Babur defeats another part of Ibrahim's army."],
          ["About a week", "The two armies face each other in front of the city of Panipat."],
          ["21 April 1526", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The battle took place at Panipat, about 80 km north of Delhi, on a plain that would later see two other decisive battles. Some accounts give 20 April as the date of the battle."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Babur, 43 years old" },
        "A soldier and a writer: his diary, the Baburnama, is one of the great memoirs of the age. He had lost his home kingdom, in Central Asia, and needed another. At Panipat, he bet on a tactic he himself called the 'Ottoman style': tying wagons in a row and placing the shooters behind them.",
        { h: "Ibrahim Lodi, about 45 years old" },
        "Born around 1480 in Delhi, he distrusted the Afghan and Turkic nobles. He imprisoned some, crushed a rebellion, and lost support. He had also been defeated by Rana Sanga, which cost him prestige. At Panipat, he had an immense army, but without gunpowder, and he waited a long time before attacking.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On Babur's side, the Ottoman gunners Ustad Ali Quli and Mustafa Rumi, who directed the cannons, and Humayun, his son. On the Lodi side, the raja Vikramaditya of Gwalior, an ally of the sultan, with whom is linked the diamond that Humayun received later in Agra."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Babur had about 12,000 men when he crossed the Indus, and, by his own account, fewer when he reached Delhi. Some of them had matchlock [[muskets|mosquete]], and there were horsemen who shot arrows at a gallop. The sources differ on the cannons: 15 to 20 according to English Wikipedia, and only two small ones, according to Babur himself, which may refer to two batteries.",
        "Ibrahim had, according to the historians Chandra and Sarkar, 50,000 to 70,000 men and about 1,000 [[war elephants|elefantes de guerra]], but no gunpowder weapons. Another count gives 30,000 infantry and 40,000 cavalry, half of them irregular. Babur wrote that there were 100,000, and there are estimates of 40,000.",
        { note: "Babur's number, 100,000, comes from someone who wanted to show the size of what he had faced. Modern historians settle between 40,000 and 70,000, and all agree that the Lodi were far more numerous." },
        { h: "The terrain and Babur's position" },
        "An open plain, which Babur prepared. In a line, he placed about 700 wagons tied to one another with rawhide ropes, in the 'Ottoman style' (the Ottomans used chains). Between them, wooden [[mantlets|mantelete]] covered the musketeers; accounts differ on how many between each pair of wagons. He left gaps 100 to 200 horsemen wide, so that the cavalry could ride out and back.",
        "The right wing rested on the city of Panipat; the left, on a ditch filled with branches, against the cavalry. He kept a [[reserve|reserva]] and placed cavalry detachments at the extreme ends of the line, ready for an envelopment in the style of the [[tulughma|tulughma]].",
        { h: "The wait" },
        "The two armies faced each other for about a week. Every day, Babur's men came close to Ibrahim's camp, shot arrows and tried to provoke an attack. Ibrahim did not take the bait. Babur then sent 4,000 to 5,000 men on a night attack, which went wrong: at dawn, they were exposed near the enemy lines. Humayun and a vanguard went to help them, and the whole army followed. Ibrahim formed up as if to pursue, but did not pursue. Later, a false alarm disturbed Babur's camp.",
        { h: "21 April" },
        { formation: true },
        "In the morning, Ibrahim left his camp and advanced against Babur's lines. But the front that Babur offered was too narrow for him to use his numbers. His army was squeezed, and the wings had no room to attack or fall back. The center, alone, beat against the wagons and could not get through.",
        "The fire of the muskets and cannons pinned the center. The noise of the cannons frightened the elephants, which increased the casualties. The detachments at the ends, Babur's cavalry, went around and struck the rear, and the left and right wings hit the flanks. Babur gives the greatest credit to his archers, who shot from the left, from the right and from behind.",
        "The hardest fighting lasted from early morning until around noon. Ibrahim died, and his body was found only in the afternoon. According to Babur, in half a day the enemy army was reduced to dust."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Lodi losses are uncertain: Babur's men counted 15,000 to 16,000 dead; the historian Chandra speaks of about 20,000, and the people of Agra, of 40,000 to 50,000. Many prisoners were taken, and thousands died in flight. Babur's losses are not known.",
        "Babur sent a force to Agra, and Humayun went with larger troops to take the city and Ibrahim's treasure. Three days after the battle, Babur entered Delhi. He found Ibrahim's mother and family and treated them well, as he did the prisoners. In Agra, Humayun received a diamond of about 186 carats, linked to the raja of Gwalior, which became known as Babur's diamond and, later, as the Koh-i-Noor.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Babur's power, at first, was limited to the Punjab, Agra and Delhi. He still had to defeat Rana Sanga and the Rajputs at Khanwa, in 1527, and the Afghans of the east at the Ghaghra river, in 1529. He died in 1530. But Panipat was the beginning of the [[Mughal|mogol]] Empire, which lasted more than three hundred years and reached its highest point with his descendants, such as Akbar and the builder of the Taj Mahal.",
        "Militarily, it is remembered as one of the first battles of the subcontinent fought with gunpowder weapons, although Babur himself gives the greatest credit to his horse archers.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: historians give 20 or 21 April 1526. English Wikipedia uses the 21st." },
        { note: "The cannons: Wikipedia says 15 to 20. Babur himself, in what he describes of the battle, mentions only two small ones. As for the Lodi, one of the sources finds little evidence that they had cannons or muskets." },
        { note: "The Koh-i-Noor: the link between the diamond given to Humayun and today's Koh-i-Noor comes from one of the sources we consulted. We did not see it confirmed in others." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  mohacs: {
    war: "Ottoman-Hungarian wars, Suleiman's campaign of 1526",
    hook: "On an August afternoon, the Hungarian cavalry charged an enemy that was still getting organized, and in two hours the medieval kingdom of Hungary ceased to exist.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the start of the 16th century, the Kingdom of Hungary was the main Christian frontier between the Ottoman Empire and Central Europe. But it was a kingdom weak from within. The king's power was small, and the mercenary army that Matthias Corvinus had created had fallen apart. In 1521, the Ottomans took Belgrade, the great fortress on the Danube.",
        "In 1522, the young king Louis II married Mary of Habsburg, which brought Hungary closer to the Habsburgs. The Ottomans saw this as a threat, and an alliance between Suleiman and France encouraged him to attack.",
        { h: "The road to Mohács" },
        { timeline: [
          ["16 April 1526", "Suleiman leaves Constantinople, according to English Wikipedia."],
          ["13 July", "The siege of Pétervárad begins; it ends in surrender."],
          ["20 July", "Louis II leaves Buda."],
          ["14 to 23 August", "The Ottomans reach the Drava river and cross it."],
          ["26 August", "Council of war: it is decided to fight at Mohács."],
          ["29 August", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The Hungarian army was gathered slowly. The nobles were slow to respond, and many contingents never arrived. There were three forces: one from Transylvania, of 8,000 to 13,000 men, under the [[voivode|voivoda]] John Zápolya; one from Croatia, of about 5,000, under Christopher Frankopan; and the main one, with the king. Zápolya's did not arrive in time. The Croatian one arrived only in part, and the ban Ferenc Batthyány appeared on the morning of the battle with about 4,000 men, roughly a third of what had been promised."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Louis II of Hungary, 20 years old" },
        "Born on 1 July 1506, he was crowned king of Hungary at almost 2 years old and king of Bohemia the following year. In 1526, he left Buda in mid-July, determined, it is said, to repel the invaders or be crushed once and for all. According to one version, he even argued for retreat at the last moment, but pressure from the nobles and the frontier troops led him to fight.",
        { h: "Pál Tomori, about 51 years old" },
        "Born around 1475, he served a noble family and built a career in castles of Transylvania, before joining the Franciscans in 1520 and becoming archbishop of Kalocsa in 1523. He was captain of the southern frontier and a skilled commander. At Mohács, he was named commander-in-chief, together with György Szapolyai, by a vote, and he was the one who actually led the attack.",
        { h: "Suleiman and the vizier Ibrahim" },
        "Suleiman I, the Magnificent, commanded the campaign in person. The [[vizier|vizir]] Pargalı Ibrahim Pasha commanded the vanguard and, in the battle, the center. Bali Bey and Hüsrev Bey, commanders of the light [[akinci|akinci]] cavalry, were sent to surround the Hungarian flanks.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Hungarian side, the chancellor Stephen Brodarics, who wrote an account of the battle and estimated the army at about 25,000 Hungarians; Ferenc Batthyány on the right wing; Péter Perényi on the left; and Lenart Gnoiński, chief of staff and leader of the Polish foot soldiers, who proposed a wagon fort, like Hunyadi's at Kosovo, an idea that was not adopted."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Hungarians numbered about 25,000, according to Brodarics, who was there, and historians' estimates run from 25,000 to 30,000. Some authors go as high as 50,000–60,000. The composition divides the sources: one list speaks of about 3,000 noble horsemen, 1,000 of the royal guard, 4,500 light cavalry, 6,700 Hungarians on foot, 5,300 German foot soldiers paid by the pope and 1,500 Poles; another speaks of about 15,000 horsemen and 10,000 infantry. The artillery: 85 cannons, of which about 53 were used.",
        "The Ottomans were considerably more, and the number is disputed. English Wikipedia gives about 45,000 combatants; Hungarian sources speak of 60,000 to 80,000, and the upper limit is 100,000, counting the whole army. Brodarics estimated the camp at 200,000, of whom 80,000 were combatants. They were [[Janissaries|janízaros]], sipahi cavalry, troops from the Balkans, and about 300 cannons.",
        { note: "The numbers have wide ranges. One of the sources speaks of Ottomans two or three times more numerous, and all give them much more artillery." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A field about 7 km south of Mohács, bounded to the east by the marshes of the Danube. To the south and west, a terrace 25 to 30 meters high (historians dispute its slope) bordered the field. A stream, the Borza, cut across it, and the ground was uneven, with ridges and valleys that limited visibility. The Hungarians stood 2 to 2.5 km from the terrace.",
        { h: "The Hungarian choices" },
        "At the council of war, the chancellor Brodarics advised waiting for reinforcements, which were days away. But impatient nobles convinced the king to fight at once. The palatine Báthori refused command because of gout, and the ones chosen were György Szapolyai, the voivode's younger brother with no command experience, and the reluctant Tomori.",
        { h: "The afternoon of 29 August" },
        { formation: true },
        "At dawn, the Hungarians formed in two lines parallel to the terrace. In the first, most of the infantry, about 10,000, in the center, with Batthyány on the right and Perényi on the left, with heavy cavalry, and the cannons spread along the line. In the second, the king, with some 3,000 heavy horsemen and the guards. The army had no depth: anything less than a perfect charge would doom it.",
        "The Ottomans marched about 12 km, almost seven hours. According to Suleiman's diary, they reached the edge of the terrace around two in the afternoon. The vizier Ibrahim came down first with the Rumelian corps, the janissaries and the artillery, and set up batteries protected by stakes. The light cavalry of Bali Bey and Hüsrev Bey was sent to the flanks. The sultan and the Anatolian corps were still behind. Suleiman ordered the Rumelians to make camp, and the Hungarians may have taken this as a sign that there would be no battle that day.",
        "Between three and four in the afternoon (the sources differ: English Wikipedia says only 'early afternoon', and the Portuguese one gives 1 p.m. and 2 p.m. for the arrival of the two Ottoman corps), Tomori went to the king, obtained approval and ordered the right wing and the center infantry to attack. He wanted to defeat the Rumelian corps before the main army formed up. The attack threw the Rumelians into confusion.",
        "The Rumelian artillery fired over the advancing horsemen, and the janissary volleys stopped the cavalry in front of the cannons. The Hungarian infantry, which needed half an hour to arrive, was late, and this gave the Ottomans time to reorganize. The light cavalry feigned a retreat, and the second Hungarian line charged. Ibrahim's center fell back in the shape of a [[crescent|crescente]], according to English Wikipedia, which exposed the flanks of the advancing cavalry; the reading that it was a planned [[feigned retreat|retirada fingida]] is that of one source, not of all.",
        "Meanwhile, the Anatolian corps formed up at the foot of the terrace, and the Ottomans became far more numerous on the field. Tomori lost contact with Perényi, and command broke down. Perényi's left wing attacked the Anatolians, who were already in order, and the janissary volleys brought it down. The infantry fought until it was destroyed, with the Ottoman light cavalry closing the exits. Tomori was killed trying to rally the fleeing cavalry. English Wikipedia says Suleiman was hit by bullets that glanced off his cuirass.",
        "It was all over in about two hours. King Louis tried to flee. While trying to climb the steep bank of the Csele stream, he fell backward off his horse, into the water, and drowned, pinned by his armor. His body was found only weeks later."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Hungarian losses exceeded 14,000 men, and estimates go up to 20,000: about 4,000 cavalry and 10,000 infantry, according to the historian Géza Perjés. More than a thousand nobles died, including seven prelates and 28 barons. About 10,000 men were captured, and some 2,000 were executed in the following days. Ottoman losses are unknown and were probably high: Portuguese Wikipedia speaks of 2,000 to 3,000, but the number has no clear source.",
        "The news reached Buda on 30 August, and Queen Mary fled. Suleiman stayed four days at Mohács and entered Buda on 11 September, where he plundered the castle and burned Buda and Pest. Bali Bey devastated the region in raids. A group of about 25,000 people, according to English Wikipedia, was massacred in a camp near Dömös. Leaving no garrison, the sultan went home and reached Constantinople in November.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In 1526, the Hungarian nobles elected two rival kings: John Zápolya and the Habsburg Ferdinand I. The Ottomans took Buda in 1541, and Hungary was divided into three parts: one under Ottoman rule, one under the Habsburgs and a semi-independent Transylvania. Ottoman rule over much of the country lasted until the end of the 17th century. In Hungarian memory, Mohács is the end of the medieval kingdom, and there is a saying: 'More was lost at Mohács.'",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: 29 August was the day in the Julian calendar. In the Gregorian, the anniversary falls on 8 September." },
        { note: "Zápolya's absence: the voivode had written to the king asking him not to fight before his arrival, and reached only Szeged, on 27 August, delayed by rain. One of the studies we consulted finds that he probably would not have arrived in time even with clear orders." },
        { note: "The tactics: the historian Géza Perjés said the Hungarian formation was 'good for nothing, except a desperate charge'. The idea that Ibrahim planned a retreat to draw in the horsemen comes from some of the sources and is disputed." },
        { note: "The sources: the closest Hungarian account is Brodarics's, and there is also Suleiman's diary. The numbers on both sides have been debated for centuries." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  cajamarca: {
    war: "Conquest of the Inca Empire by Francisco Pizarro (1532–1572)",
    hook: "On a November afternoon, 168 Spaniards captured an emperor in the midst of thousands of men and, in half an hour, decided the fate of the greatest empire in the Americas.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Inca Empire, the largest in the Americas before the arrival of the Europeans, stretched across the Andes. In 1532, it was emerging from a civil war between two half-brothers, Huáscar and Atahualpa. Atahualpa was captured at Tumebamba, escaped, won several battles and, in the last, at Quipaipán, in April 1532, took Huáscar prisoner. War and disease had weakened the empire.",
        "It was at this moment that the Spaniards arrived. Francisco Pizarro, the illegitimate son of a poor family from Trujillo, in Extremadura, never learned to read. He had gone to the Americas in 1509 and, in 1524, partnered with Diego de Almagro and Hernando de Luque to conquer what lay south of Panama. He tried twice. In 1529, King Charles I named him governor of the lands he might conquer. He left Panama at the end of 1530 and, in 1532, founded the city of San Miguel de Piura.",
        { h: "The road to Cajamarca" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1529 to 1532", "Inca civil war between Huáscar and Atahualpa."],
          ["July 1529", "The king of Spain names Pizarro governor of the lands to be conquered."],
          ["February 1532", "Pizarro reaches Tumbes and finds it partly destroyed by the civil war."],
          ["April 1532", "Atahualpa wins at Quipaipán and takes Huáscar prisoner."],
          ["15 November 1532", "The Spaniards reach Cajamarca."],
          ["16 November 1532", "The battle, or, as many historians prefer, the massacre."]
        ]},
        "Pizarro climbed the Andes with about 168 men: 106 on foot and 62 on horseback. Atahualpa could have ambushed them in the mountains. He let them come. His army was camped at Pultumarca, about a league from Cajamarca, today Baños del Inca. The city, of some 2,000 inhabitants, was almost empty."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Francisco Pizarro, nearly 55 years old" },
        "His year of birth is uncertain, between 1475 and 1478. With 168 men, he had no way of beating the Inca army in open country. His plan was surprise: to lead the emperor into an enclosed plaza and capture him at one stroke. Without him, the empire, where everything depended on the emperor, would be left without direction.",
        { h: "Atahualpa, about 30 years old" },
        "He had just won the civil war and was at his peak. He agreed to visit the Spaniards at Cajamarca. Why, historians debate: perhaps he was overconfident, perhaps he thought he could control them. He did not imagine that 168 men could be dangerous to a crowd of thousands.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Hernando Pizarro, Francisco's brother, and Hernando de Soto commanded the cavalry; Juan Pizarro, another brother, commanded part of the infantry. Soto went to Atahualpa's camp, read a speech through an interpreter and put on a riding display. The friar Vicente de Valverde, with the interpreter Felipillo (or Martinillo, depending on the chronicler), read the [[Requerimiento|requerimento]]. Pedro de Candía stayed in a tower with a [[falconet|falconete]]. On the Inca side, the general Rumiñahui waited outside the city with about 8,000 men."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Spaniards numbered 168, according to most sources, although 180 and 175 are also mentioned. There were some [[arquebuses|arcabuz]], about 12, and small firearms: the sources speak of two, three or four cannons.",
        "The Inca army, at Pultumarca, is given as about 80,000 men by John Hemming, and some 30,000 by Spanish Wikipedia. But what mattered was what entered the plaza. Atahualpa arrived with a retinue of 3,000 to 8,000 people, according to modern historians. Jared Diamond says they were servants and nobles, and John Michael Francis, guards with ceremonial weapons. The old chroniclers spoke of 30,000 to 40,000. Those who were armed had, according to one source, small decorative axes.",
        { note: "Atahualpa's retinue is one of the most disputed numbers: from 3,000 to 40,000, depending on the source. What nearly everyone accepts is that they were far more than the Spaniards, and that they had almost no weapons." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The plaza of Cajamarca was a large open space, surrounded by buildings where the Spaniards hid. There was a tower over the plaza.",
        { h: "The ambush" },
        "Pizarro divided the cavalry between Hernando Pizarro and Soto, and put bells on the horses. He divided the infantry between himself and Juan Pizarro. Candía went to the tower with three soldiers, two trumpeters and two falconets, one of them out of order. The Spaniards did not know that the Incas did not usually fight at night, and the wait, with Atahualpa's slow procession, made them nervous. Pedro Pizarro says that many urinated out of fear.",
        "Atahualpa arrived near nightfall, in a [[litter|liteira]] covered with parrot feathers and partly with silver, carried by some 80 nobles, with other litters behind. He was surprised not to see any Spaniard in the plaza.",
        "Valverde, with the interpreter, stepped forward and read the [[Requerimiento|requerimento]]: the Inca was to accept the Christian faith and King Charles I as sovereign. Atahualpa refused to be a tributary. The sources differ on what came next. In the best-known version, he asked to see Valverde's book (a breviary or a Bible), could not read it and threw it to the ground. Jerez says he asked to see the book; the only Inca account, by Titu Cusi Yupanqui, dictated decades later, mentions no book at all. Valverde ran to Pizarro and asked him to attack.",
        "The trumpets sounded, and Candía fired the falconet at the crowd. The cavalry charged shouting 'Santiago', and the arquebusiers fired. The retinue, almost unarmed, panicked, and the crowd knocked down a wall to flee. The Spaniards aimed at the nobles and the [[curacas|curaca]]. They cut off the hands of the litter bearers, and the litter fell. Atahualpa was pulled out. Pizarro stopped a soldier from killing him and was wounded in the hand. The fight lasted about half an hour. Rumiñahui's army, a short distance away, dispersed, and there is no proof that the Incas fought the Spaniards afterward."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The figures for Inca dead vary widely: 2,000 (English and Portuguese Wikipedia), 2,000 to 3,000 dead and 7,000 captured (English infobox), 4,000 to 5,000 dead and some 7,000 wounded or captured (Spanish Wikipedia), and 6,000 to 7,000 (Gonzalo Portocarrero). On the Spanish side, most sources speak of no dead; Wikipedia gives one Black slave dead and one Spaniard wounded, and others speak of up to five dead.",
        "The next day, the Spaniards plundered the Inca camp. Atahualpa offered to fill a room once with gold and twice with silver, in two months. The room measured about 6.7 by 5.2 meters, with a height of 2.4. The gold began to arrive from Cusco on 20 December 1532, and by 3 May 1533 Pizarro had received everything, which was melted into bars. Hemming argues that Atahualpa offered the ransom to save his life, and that no early chronicler records a promise to free him.",
        "Pizarro staged a trial, while Soto was away looking for a hidden army that did not exist. The charges: rebellion against the Spaniards, idolatry, the death of Huáscar, and, according to some accounts, polygamy and incest. The sentence was burning at the stake. Valverde proposed that, if he accepted baptism, he would be strangled. Atahualpa was baptized, with the name Francisco, and executed by [[garrote]] on 26 July 1533, according to the Wikipedia article about him. Other texts give 29 August.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "On 15 November 1533, Pizarro entered Cusco, the heart of the empire, with about 500 Spaniards. Manco Inca was placed on the throne as a puppet. The complete conquest took some forty years: the last Inca, Túpac Amaru, was captured and executed in 1572. The capture of Atahualpa left headless an empire in which everything depended on the emperor, and which civil war and disease had already weakened.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: many historians prefer 'massacre of Cajamarca' to 'battle', because on the Inca side there was almost no fighting." },
        { note: "The book on the ground: it is the most famous scene, but it depends on the Spanish chroniclers. The account of Titu Cusi Yupanqui, the only Inca one, does not mention it. English Wikipedia proposes two explanations: little importance for the Incas, or confusion with the day before." },
        { note: "The execution: Wikipedia gives 26 July 1533 as the date, and notes that some sources say 29 August, a date that later research considers wrong." },
        { note: "The sources: Francisco de Jerez, Miguel de Estete and Pedro Pizarro were Spaniards and wrote from their own point of view. Juan de Betanzos interviewed Inca guards, and the only Inca account, that of Titu Cusi Yupanqui, was dictated decades later." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

